@Tutorial(time: 20) {
    @Intro(title: "Architect for growth") {
        The task app is production-polished — and every line of it knows
        every other line. That is fine at one screen; it is what makes the
        tenth screen expensive. This chapter refactors the app around three
        old ideas in their terminal shape: a service layer for side
        effects, command/query separation for the store, and MVVM's rule
        that views make no decisions. The app looks identical after every
        step — the diffs are the lesson.

        Continue from <doc:MakeItProduction>'s finished code, or
        [download that code](https://dardevelin.github.io/terminal-ui/downloads/tasks-chapter-6.zip)
        as the base to edit.

        @Image(source: "demo-ch7.gif", alt: "The refactored app loading through its service, then toggling tasks — behavior unchanged, structure earned.")
    }

    @Section(title: "Put side effects behind a service") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            A service is the app's only door to the outside world. Today
            the "outside world" is a four-second sleep and three hardcoded
            tasks living inside a `.task` closure; the day it becomes a
            file or an HTTP call, every view that knows about the sleep is
            a view you have to edit. A `TaskService` protocol makes that
            knowledge swappable — and because the store receives its
            service through the initializer, a test can hand it a canned
            one and drive every mutation without a terminal, a delay, or a
            file in sight. That is the same seam this framework uses on
            itself: behavior worth trusting is behavior you can exercise
            from a plain test target.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Define `TaskService` — `load()` and `save(_:)`, nothing
                more — and move the seed data and the simulated latency
                into `InMemoryTaskService`. The protocol is `Sendable`
                because services are called from async work; making the
                protocol carry that requirement means every future
                implementation proves it too. The store takes
                `init(service: any TaskService)`, keeps it private, and
                gains one async command, `load()`, that asks the service
                and lands the result; mutations now end with
                `service.save(tasks)` — the in-memory save has nowhere to
                write, which is honest, and exactly where a file-backed
                service would.

                The view's `.task` shrinks to `await store.load()` — it no
                longer knows where tasks come from or how long they take.
                Only the spinner ticker stays behind, on the loading row
                itself: animation is presentation, so it belongs to the
                view, and it stops when the load lands.

                @Code(name: "main.swift", file: "tasks7-01-01.swift") {
                    @Image(source: "preview-tasks7-01-01.png", alt: "The app after the service refactor: tasks loaded through the service, the first one toggled done.")
                }
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Commands write, queries read") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            The store's surface has grown organically: mutations, derived
            values, and helpers in the order they were needed. This step
            reorganizes it around one rule — CQRS in its lightweight,
            single-process form. Commands (`addDraft`, `toggle(id:)`,
            `deleteSelected`, `moveSelection`) are the only code anywhere
            that writes state, and each is named for the user's intent.
            Queries (`visibleTasks`, `remaining`, `completion`,
            `selectedTask`) are the only surface views render, each
            derived fact computed in exactly one place. Handlers call
            commands; bodies read queries. The one exception is a control's
            binding — the prompt's draft, the selected tab, the Settings
            toggle — which writes the property it edits, as a SwiftUI control
            does; a binding is a command the framework calls for you.

            Not a line of behavior changes. What changes is what you can
            answer by reading: "what can mutate the task list?" is now a
            one-section grep instead of a whole-program audit.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Regroup the store under two `MARK` headers and add the two
                queries the views were computing inline: `selectedTask`
                replaces the `first(where:)` in the detail pane (and the
                `contains` gate in `requestDelete`), and `completion`
                names what the header's `remaining == 0` check was really
                asking — an empty list counts as vacuously complete, so
                the answer is bit-identical to the old one.

                Run it and nothing is different, which is the point: this
                is the refactor you make while the app is small so the
                rule is already standing when it is not. At one screen the
                discipline is optional; at ten screens, with three stores,
                it is the difference between a codebase and a crime scene.

                @Code(name: "main.swift", file: "tasks7-02-01.swift") {
                    @Image(source: "preview-tasks7-02-01.png", alt: "The app after the command/query split, rendering identically: the second task selected and toggled, the detail pane following.")
                }
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Views stay dumb") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Map the vocabulary onto what you have and MVVM turns out to be
            already half-built: `TaskItem` and `TaskService` are the
            model, the `@Observable` `TaskStore` is the view-model, and
            every ``TerminalView`` struct is the view. The remaining leak
            is decision-making in view bodies — `TaskRow` chooses glyphs
            and colors, the header runs a three-way `if`. Bodies re-run on
            every observed change, so logic in a body is logic that runs
            per frame, invisible to tests, and duplicated the first time a
            second view needs the same answer. The fix is to hand views
            conclusions, not questions.

            The same step pays off the service seam: the composition root
            swaps `InMemoryTaskService` for a JSON-backed
            `FileTaskService` — one changed line at the root, plus
            `Codable` on `TaskItem` so it can be written to disk — and your
            tasks now survive a relaunch. That is the whole
            argument for injection, demonstrated: the day you want a real
            backend, the store and every view are already ready.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Add two presentation structs. `TaskRowPresentation` is
                everything a row shows — cursor, checkbox, both colors —
                built by the `row(for:)` query; `StatusLight` is a label
                whose ``Color`` carries its meaning, produced by
                `statusLight` for the header and `stateLight(for:)` for
                the detail pane. `TaskRow`'s body loses its last
                conditional, and the header's `if`/`else` chain collapses
                into one `StatusText` line.

                Every choice the UI makes is now a named query you can pin
                with `#expect`; the views place answers on screen and do
                nothing else. The app renders the same pixels it did three
                steps ago — but the next screen you add starts from parts
                that already know their jobs.

                @Code(name: "main.swift", file: "tasks7-03-01.swift") {
                    @Image(source: "preview-tasks7-03-01.png", alt: "The finished app rendering through presentation structs: the last task selected and done, the status light showing two open.")
                }
            }
        }
    }

    @Assessments {
        @MultipleChoice {
            Why does the store take its `TaskService` through the initializer instead of constructing `InMemoryTaskService` itself?

            @Choice(isCorrect: true) {
                The store only knows the protocol, so tests can inject a
                canned service and exercise every command without a
                terminal, a delay, or a real data source.

                @Justification(reaction: "Right.") {
                    Injection moves the choice of implementation to the
                    one construction site. The store becomes testable from
                    a plain test target — the same seam the framework uses
                    for its own behavior.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                Constructing a service inside an `@Observable` class is
                not allowed.

                @Justification(reaction: "Not quite.") {
                    The store could build the service itself just fine —
                    it would compile and run. It would also weld the store
                    to one implementation, which is what injection undoes.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                Initializer injection makes `load()` run faster.

                @Justification(reaction: "Not quite.") {
                    Performance is identical either way. What injection
                    buys is substitutability — for tests today, for a
                    file-backed service tomorrow.
                }
            }
        }

        @MultipleChoice {
            Under the command/query rule, what is allowed to call `deleteSelected()`?

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                Any view body that notices the list should shrink.

                @Justification(reaction: "Not quite.") {
                    Bodies render queries; they never call commands. A
                    body that mutated state would trigger another render
                    from inside a render.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: true) {
                Event handlers — `onEvent`, an `AlertAction`, an
                `onClick` — which are the only places user intent arrives.

                @Justification(reaction: "Right.") {
                    Commands are the only writers, and handlers are the
                    only callers of commands. Bodies read queries, and
                    nothing else touches state — that is the whole rule.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                A query, so views can trigger the delete by reading it.

                @Justification(reaction: "Not quite.") {
                    A query that mutated state would make every render a
                    potential write — the exact ambiguity the separation
                    exists to remove. Queries stay read-only.
                }
            }
        }

        @MultipleChoice {
            Why must view bodies stay logic-free?

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                Result builders cannot compile conditionals.

                @Justification(reaction: "Not quite.") {
                    They can — the app has used `if` in bodies since
                    chapter one. The reason is about where decisions
                    live, not about what compiles.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: true) {
                Bodies re-run on every observed change, so body logic runs
                per frame, cannot be tested directly, and gets duplicated
                the first time another view needs the same decision.

                @Justification(reaction: "Right.") {
                    Moving the decision into a query names it, computes it
                    once per frame in one place, and makes it testable
                    with `#expect` — the view just places the answer.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                Logic in a body blocks the render thread and drops frames.

                @Justification(reaction: "Not quite.") {
                    A ternary in a body will not drop a frame. The cost is
                    architectural — unnamed, untestable, duplicated
                    decisions — and it compounds with screen count, not
                    with CPU.
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
